.TH FACIOLOCKCTL 1 "2026-08-31" "faciolock 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
faciolockctl \- enrol, test and diagnose Faciolock face authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B faciolockctl
.RI [ --config " PATH" ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B faciolockctl
is the administrative front end to
.BR faciolockd (8).
It talks to the daemon over
.IR /run/faciolock.sock ;
it holds no templates and does no inference of its own.
.PP
Every command except
.B diagnose
requires root, because the socket is mode 0660 and the template store is 0700.
.I USER
defaults to
.BR $SUDO_USER ,
so
.B sudo faciolockctl enroll
does the obvious thing.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI diagnose " " [ --write ]
Report what this machine has: which
.I /dev/video*
node is the infrared camera, what it delivers, and which emitter tier it is.
With
.BR --write ,
record the result in
.IR /etc/faciolock/faciolockd.toml .
It refuses to overwrite an existing config rather than clobbering an
administrator's file, so remove it first if you mean to re-run this.
This is the only command that does not need root.
.TP
.BI enroll " " [ USER ]
Guided enrolment. Three poses, prompted one at a time. Needs a room dark enough
that the camera's own infrared emitter is what is lighting the face; see
.B LIMITATIONS
below.
.B enrol
is accepted as a spelling of this.
.TP
.B list
Who is enrolled.
.TP
.BI remove " USER " [ --yes ]
Delete an enrolment. Prompts unless
.B --yes
is given.
.TP
.BI test " " [ USER ]
Perform exactly one authentication, asking the daemon the same question PAM
would ask. This is the safe way to confirm the whole path works, because it
touches no file under
.IR /etc/pam.d .
It also clears a spent failure budget on the way out.
.TP
.BI bench " " [ -n " N" "] [" USER ]
Run N authentications and report p50, p95 and p99. The first sample is a
warm-up and is not counted, because a cold daemon start would otherwise be the
p99 at small N.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --config " PATH"
Read the daemon configuration from
.I PATH
instead of
.IR /etc/faciolock/faciolockd.toml .
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Usage summary.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/faciolock/faciolockd.toml
Daemon configuration. Absent on a fresh install, and that is the designed state:
both the daemon and this tool read its absence as "use the defaults".
.TP
.I /var/lib/faciolock/
Encrypted templates, their key, the failure budget and the audit log. Mode 0700,
owned by the
.B faciolock
system user.
.TP
.I /run/faciolock.sock
The daemon's socket, mode 0660, group
.BR faciolock-auth .
.SH LIMITATIONS
Two are measured rather than suspected, and both ship with this version.
.PP
In daylight the emitter must still be visible over ambient infrared: every
frame pair needs OFF/ON at most 0.9 and ON\-OFF at least 5 levels. A weak,
irregular or saturated capture is refused and authentication falls through to
a password. Recorded daylight captures pass; a daylight run on hardware has not
been observed at this version.
.PP
A flat printed photograph is not reliably rejected. There is currently no
enforced liveness check that separates a print from a live face.
.PP
Face authentication is a convenience factor, not a password replacement.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, non-zero on failure. A refused face is a failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pam_faciolock (8),
.BR pam-auth-update (8),
.BR sysusers.d (5)
.PP
.I /usr/share/doc/faciolock/README.Debian
for setup, and
.I /usr/share/doc/libpam-faciolock/README.Debian
for enabling face authentication and for the recovery path if it misbehaves.
